DEEP[1]=("Chapter 9 is named Rāja-Vidyā Rāja-Guhya Yoga, the yoga of the royal knowledge and royal secret. Many devotees consider it the heart of the Gītā’s teaching on bhakti; it ends with the verse that returns as 18.65.",
 "<i>Idaṃ tu te guhyatamaṃ</i>, “this most secret”. <i>Pravakṣyāmy anasūyave</i>, “I will declare to you, who do not carp”. <i>Jñānaṃ vijñāna-sahitaṃ</i>, “knowledge together with realisation”. <i>Yaj jñātvā mokṣyase’śubhāt</i>, “knowing which you will be freed from ill”. Commentators note the steps of secrecy in the Gītā: <i>guhya</i>, secret (18.63); <i>guhyatara</i>, more secret; <i>guhyatama</i>, most secret (9.1, 15.20); <i>sarva-guhyatama</i>, most secret of all (18.64). Śaṅkara says the secret here is knowledge of Brahman. Rāmānuja says it is bhakti, devotion, in which the Lord gives himself. <i>Anasūya</i>, one who does not find fault, is the right listener.",
 "Deep things are shared with those who listen openly. Come to teachings without suspicion, and more will open.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Rāja-vidyā rāja-guhyaṃ</i>, “king of sciences, king of secrets”. <i>Pavitram idam uttamam</i>, “supreme purifier”. <i>Pratyakṣāvagamaṃ dharmyaṃ</i>, “directly experienced, righteous”. <i>Su-sukhaṃ kartum avyayam</i>, “very easy to do, imperishable”.",
 "Seven qualities in one verse. Commentators linger on two. <i>Pratyakṣāvagama</i>, directly experienced: unlike heaven promised after death, the fruits of devotion are felt now, as peace and joy. <i>Su-sukha</i>, very easy: one need not be learned, rich or ascetic to love. Śaṅkara says the knowledge is easy because it is the knowledge of one’s own Self. Rāmānuja says bhakti is easy because the Lord himself helps. Some read <i>rāja</i> also as “for kings”: a teaching fit for those in the world.",
 "The deepest practices are often the simplest. Love, gratitude and offering are open to anyone today.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Aśraddadhānāḥ puruṣā</i>, “people without faith”. <i>Dharmasyāsya parantapa</i>, “in this dharma, scorcher of foes”. <i>Aprāpya māṃ nivartante</i>, “not reaching me, return”. <i>Mṛtyu-saṃsāra-vartmani</i>, “to the path of death and rebirth”.",
 "<i>Mṛtyu-saṃsāra-vartman</i>, “the road of death and wandering”, is a stark phrase for the cycle of rebirth. Commentators explain <i>śraddhā</i> here not as blind belief but as willingness to trust and try. Śaṅkara says those without faith remain in the cycle because they do not practise the means. Rāmānuja says they turn away from the easy path offered. Chapter 17 (17.3) will say a person is made of their faith: <i>yo yac-chraddhaḥ sa eva saḥ</i>.",
 "A little trust is needed to begin anything worthwhile. Give a good path a real try before deciding.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Mayā tatam idaṃ sarvaṃ</i>, “by me all this is pervaded”. <i>Jagad avyakta-mūrtinā</i>, “the world, by my unmanifest form”. <i>Mat-sthāni sarva-bhūtāni</i>, “all beings dwell in me”. <i>Na cāhaṃ teṣv avasthitaḥ</i>, “but I do not dwell in them”.",
 "Verses 4–6 form one of the Gītā’s great paradoxes. Commentators of every school treat them carefully. Śaṅkara: beings exist in the Lord as appearances, but the Lord, being the reality, is not contained in them. Rāmānuja: beings depend on the Lord for existence, but the Lord does not depend on them. He supports them as the soul supports the body. Madhva: the Lord is independent; all else depends on him. The image of space is common: everything exists in space, but space is not bounded by things. Compare 7.12: “they are in me, I am not in them”.",
 "Everything depends on something larger. Remembering that can ease the burden of feeling we hold it all.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Na ca mat-sthāni bhūtāni</i>, “and yet beings do not dwell in me”. <i>Paśya me yogam aiśvaram</i>, “see my divine yoga”. <i>Bhūta-bhṛn na ca bhūta-stho</i>, “sustaining beings, not dwelling in them”. <i>Mamātmā bhūta-bhāvanaḥ</i>, “my Self is the source of beings”.",
 "Kṛṣṇa now seems to contradict 9.4: beings are not in him either. Commentators delight in this. <i>Paśya me yogam aiśvaram</i>, “behold my divine power”: Kṛṣṇa calls it a mystery to be seen, not merely reasoned. Śaṅkara’s famous image is the rope and snake: in dim light a rope looks like a snake; the snake is “in” the rope only as appearance. Rāmānuja: beings are not in the Lord in the sense of limiting or touching him; he holds them by his will. The phrase <i>aiśvara yoga</i>, divine power, returns in 11.8, when Arjuna is given divine sight.",
 "Some truths are better wondered at than solved. Let a little mystery remain.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Yathākāśa-sthito nityaṃ</i>, “as, always resting in space”. <i>Vāyuḥ sarvatra-go mahān</i>, “the mighty wind moving everywhere”. <i>Tathā sarvāṇi bhūtāni</i>, “so all beings”. <i>Mat-sthānīty upadhāraya</i>, “rest in me, understand”.",
 "The wind-and-space image helps with the paradox. Wind is always in space, moves freely, can be violent, yet never touches or changes space. Commentators note how apt the image is: we never see space directly; we see what is in it. Śaṅkara says beings exist in the Lord without attachment, as wind in space. Rāmānuja says the Lord holds beings by his will, untouched. Many find this image calming: however stormy life is, it moves in a vast stillness that is never disturbed.",
 "However stormy today is, it moves within a vast, quiet space. Notice the space as well as the storm.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Sarva-bhūtāni kaunteya</i>, “all beings, son of Kuntī”. <i>Prakṛtiṃ yānti māmikām</i>, “enter my nature”. <i>Kalpa-kṣaye punas tāni</i>, “at the end of a kalpa, again them”. <i>Kalpādau visṛjāmy aham</i>, “at the start of a kalpa, I send forth”.",
 "<i>Kalpa</i>, a day of Brahmā (8.17), is the unit of cosmic creation. Commentators note that this continues 8.18–19 but now speaks of the Lord’s own nature, <i>prakṛti māmikā</i>, as the womb into which beings return. Śaṅkara calls it the Lord’s lower nature, made of the guṇas. Rāmānuja calls it subtle matter, part of the Lord’s body. The cycle is not chaos but rhythm, held in the Lord. The Purāṇas describe these cycles in great detail.",
 "Endings and beginnings belong to one rhythm. What closes now prepares what opens next.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Prakṛtiṃ svām avaṣṭabhya</i>, “resting on my own nature”. <i>Visṛjāmi punaḥ punaḥ</i>, “I send forth again and again”. <i>Bhūta-grāmam imaṃ kṛtsnam</i>, “this whole host of beings”. <i>Avaśaṃ prakṛter vaśāt</i>, “helpless under the sway of nature”.",
 "Two kinds of power are contrasted. The Lord is <i>sva-vaśa</i>, free, acting through his own nature. Beings are <i>avaśa</i>, helpless, carried by nature and karma. Commentators note the repeated <i>punaḥ punaḥ</i>, “again and again”: creation is ongoing. Śaṅkara says beings are helpless because of ignorance and its products. Rāmānuja says they are helpless because of karma, which the Lord honours. The verse does not blame beings; it describes their situation, and the rest of the chapter offers the way out: devotion.",
 "Much of what we do is habit. Freedom grows as we notice our habits and choose consciously.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Na ca māṃ tāni karmāṇi</i>, “and those actions do not”. <i>Nibadhnanti dhanañjaya</i>, “bind me, Dhanañjaya”. <i>Udāsīnavad āsīnam</i>, “sitting as one indifferent”. <i>Asaktaṃ teṣu karmasu</i>, “unattached to those actions”.",
 "<i>Udāsīna</i>, literally “sitting above”, means neutral or uninvolved, like a judge. Commentators stress that it does not mean uncaring; the Lord is the friend of all beings (5.29). It means unattached: he acts without needing anything from the act. Śaṅkara says the Lord is not bound because he has no sense of doership. Rāmānuja says he is not bound because his actions are for the good of beings, according to their karma. The Lord models the detached action asked of Arjuna in 3.22–25.",
 "Act fully, but don’t let the action own you. Even the Creator, in this view, works this way.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Mayādhyakṣeṇa prakṛtiḥ</i>, “with me as overseer, nature”. <i>Sūyate sa-carācaram</i>, “produces the moving and unmoving”. <i>Hetunānena kaunteya</i>, “for this reason, son of Kuntī”. <i>Jagad viparivartate</i>, “the world revolves”.",
 "<i>Adhyakṣa</i>, overseer or presiding witness. Commentators often use the sun as an image: plants grow, water evaporates, life flourishes in the sun’s presence, yet the sun does not “do” each act. Śaṅkara says the Lord, as pure consciousness, is the witness in whose presence nature works. Rāmānuja says the Lord directs nature by his will. The Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (6.11) calls the Lord <i>karmādhyakṣa</i>, “overseer of actions”, and <i>sākṣī</i>, witness. The verse explains how the world turns without binding the Lord.",
 "Calm presence can be powerful. A steady person in a room changes the room without doing much.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Avajānanti māṃ mūḍhā</i>, “the deluded scorn me”. <i>Mānuṣīṃ tanum āśritam</i>, “having taken a human form”. <i>Paraṃ bhāvam ajānanto</i>, “not knowing my higher nature”. <i>Mama bhūta-maheśvaram</i>, “as the great Lord of beings”.",
 "In the Mahābhārata, Śiśupāla insulted Kṛṣṇa a hundred times at Yudhiṣṭhira’s great sacrifice, calling him a cowherd unworthy of honour. Duryodhana tried to arrest Kṛṣṇa when he came as a peace envoy. Commentators use such stories to illustrate the verse. Śaṅkara says the deluded see only the body. Rāmānuja says they think the Lord’s accessibility in human form is a weakness, when it is his greatest grace. The verse echoes 7.24–25. The Divine often comes humbly; it takes eyes to see.",
 "Don’t judge by appearances. The greatest may come in the plainest form.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Moghāśā mogha-karmāṇo</i>, “of vain hopes, vain actions”. <i>Mogha-jñānā vicetasaḥ</i>, “vain knowledge, senseless”. <i>Rākṣasīm āsurīṃ caiva</i>, “the rākṣasa and asura”. <i>Prakṛtiṃ mohinīṃ śritāḥ</i>, “deluding nature, they resort to”.",
 "<i>Mogha</i>, futile or empty, is repeated three times. Commentators explain: hopes, actions and knowledge cut off from their source lead nowhere lasting. <i>Rākṣasa</i> nature is cruel and violent; <i>āsura</i> nature is proud and selfish. Chapter 16 describes both in detail (16.4–20). Śaṅkara says those of such nature harm others and themselves. Rāmānuja says they reject the Lord’s grace. The verse is a warning, not a curse; chapter 9 will soon say that even the worst can turn (9.30).",
 "Hopes rooted in something deeper last longer. Check where your hopes are planted.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Mahātmānas tu māṃ pārtha</i>, “but the great souls, Pārtha”. <i>Daivīṃ prakṛtim āśritāḥ</i>, “sheltered in the divine nature”. <i>Bhajanty ananya-manaso</i>, “worship with undivided mind”. <i>Jñātvā bhūtādim avyayam</i>, “knowing me as the imperishable origin of beings”.",
 "<i>Daivī prakṛti</i>, divine nature, is described in 16.1–3: fearlessness, purity, generosity, self-control, kindness and more. Commentators note that the <i>mahātmā</i> is defined by devotion and character, not by status or power. Śaṅkara says the great souls have a sāttvika nature and worship the Lord as the Self of all. Rāmānuja says they cannot live without the Lord, loving him above all. The contrast with 9.11–12 is deliberate: the same human form of Kṛṣṇa is scorned by some and adored by others.",
 "Greatness of soul shows in steady love and good character, not in fame or power.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Satataṃ kīrtayanto māṃ</i>, “always singing my praises”. <i>Yatantaś ca dṛḍha-vratāḥ</i>, “striving with firm vows”. <i>Namasyantaś ca māṃ bhaktyā</i>, “bowing to me with devotion”. <i>Nitya-yuktā upāsate</i>, “ever united, they worship”.",
 "<i>Kīrtana</i>, singing the Lord’s names and glories, became a great tradition in India. The Bhakti saints made it a path open to all: the Ālvārs and Nāyanārs in Tamil, Jñāneśvar, Nāmdev and Tukārām in Marathi, Purandaradāsa in Kannada, Mīrā, Sūrdās and Tulsīdās in the north, Caitanya in Bengal. Commentators note the three-fold devotion of the verse: speech (singing), will (effort and vows), body (bowing). Rāmānuja says such devotees cannot bear even a moment without remembering the Lord.",
 "Sing, even if only in your heart. Strive, even a little. Bow, even inwardly. Devotion uses every part of us.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Jñāna-yajñena cāpy anye</i>, “others, by the sacrifice of knowledge”. <i>Yajanto mām upāsate</i>, “worship me”. <i>Ekatvena pṛthaktvena</i>, “as the One, as distinct”. <i>Bahudhā viśvato-mukham</i>, “as manifold, facing every direction”.",
 "Commentators read three ways of seeing the Lord. <i>Ekatvena</i>, as one: the non-dual vision, “I am He”. <i>Pṛthaktvena</i>, as distinct: the dual vision, “He is my Lord, I his servant”. <i>Bahudhā</i>, as manifold: seeing the Lord in many forms and in all things. Śaṅkara favours the first but includes the others. Rāmānuja reads all three as aspects of the one Lord with the world as his body. Madhva emphasises the second. The verse is remarkable for embracing different philosophies as valid forms of worship.",
 "There is room for many ways of seeing the Divine. Be curious about views different from your own.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Ahaṃ kratur ahaṃ yajñaḥ</i>, “I am the ritual, I am the sacrifice”. <i>Svadhāham aham auṣadham</i>, “I am the offering to ancestors, I am the herb”. <i>Mantro’ham aham evājyam</i>, “I am the mantra, I am the ghee”. <i>Aham agnir ahaṃ hutam</i>, “I am the fire, I am the oblation”.",
 "Each word names a part of Vedic ritual. <i>Kratu</i> is the Vedic rite itself; <i>yajña</i>, sacrifice more broadly; <i>svadhā</i>, the offering to ancestors; <i>auṣadha</i>, herb or grain, also medicine; <i>mantra</i>, the sacred formula; <i>ājya</i>, clarified butter; <i>agni</i>, fire; <i>huta</i>, the act of offering. Commentators see a parallel with 4.24 (brahmārpaṇam): every element of worship is the Lord. <i>Auṣadha</i>, healing herb, is often noted: healing too is divine. Some doctors find this verse meaningful for their work.",
 "Every element of a good act, the tools, the words, the effort, can be held as sacred.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Pitāham asya jagato</i>, “I am the father of this world”. <i>Mātā dhātā pitāmahaḥ</i>, “the mother, the supporter, the grandsire”. <i>Vedyaṃ pavitram oṃkāra</i>, “the knowable, the purifier, Oṃ”. <i>Ṛk sāma yajur eva ca</i>, “and the Ṛg, Sāma and Yajur”.",
 "Commentators find this verse tender. The Lord is father, mother, supporter and grandparent: every kind of parental love. <i>Dhātṛ</i> may mean the one who sustains or the one who dispenses fruits of action. Śaṅkara reads <i>pitāmaha</i> as the father of Brahmā, the creator. The three Vedas are named; the Atharva Veda is not, perhaps because it was later grouped with them. A popular Sanskrit prayer begins <i>tvam eva mātā ca pitā tvam eva</i>, “you alone are mother and father”, echoing this verse.",
 "Whatever love you longed for from parents, the Divine holds in full. Lean on that.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Gatir bhartā prabhuḥ sākṣī</i>, “goal, supporter, Lord, witness”. <i>Nivāsaḥ śaraṇaṃ suhṛt</i>, “home, refuge, friend”. <i>Prabhavaḥ pralayaḥ sthānaṃ</i>, “origin, dissolution, ground”. <i>Nidhānaṃ bījam avyayam</i>, “treasure-house, imperishable seed”.",
 "Twelve names of the Lord. Commentators linger on each. <i>Gati</i>, the goal and the way. <i>Bhartṛ</i>, the one who carries and nourishes. <i>Prabhu</i>, the master. <i>Sākṣin</i>, the witness of all. <i>Nivāsa</i>, the home where all dwell. <i>Śaraṇa</i>, refuge, the protector from fear. <i>Suhṛt</i>, the friend who helps without expecting return (5.29). <i>Prabhava</i> and <i>pralaya</i>, origin and end. <i>Sthāna</i>, the ground. <i>Nidhāna</i>, the treasure-house where all rests in dissolution. <i>Bīja</i>, the seed (7.10). Many recite this verse daily in prayer.",
 "Read the twelve names slowly. Pick the one you most need today, and rest in it.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Tapāmy aham ahaṃ varṣaṃ</i>, “I give heat; I the rain”. <i>Nigṛhṇāmy utsṛjāmi ca</i>, “I hold back and send forth”. <i>Amṛtaṃ caiva mṛtyuś ca</i>, “immortality and death”. <i>Sad asac cāham arjuna</i>, “being and non-being am I, Arjuna”.",
 "The verse includes opposites: heat and rain, holding and releasing, immortality and death, being and non-being. Commentators note that the Gītā does not hide the hard parts of life. Śaṅkara says the Lord as the sun dries the earth and as the cloud gives rain. Rāmānuja says all states, pleasant and unpleasant, depend on the Lord. <i>Sat</i> and <i>asat</i> are read as the manifest and unmanifest, or effect and cause. Chapter 11 will show this vividly: the Lord as Time who destroys (11.32).",
 "Both gifts and losses come from the same source. Seeing that can bring a deeper acceptance.")
DEEP[20]=("<i>Trai-vidyā māṃ soma-pāḥ</i>, “knowers of the three Vedas, drinkers of soma”. <i>Pūta-pāpā yajñair iṣṭvā</i>, “purified of sin, worshipping by sacrifices”. <i>Svar-gatiṃ prārthayante</i>, “pray for the way to heaven”. <i>Te puṇyam āsādya surendra-lokam</i>, “reaching the holy world of Indra”.",
 "<i>Soma</i> was a sacred drink used in major Vedic sacrifices, praised throughout the Ṛg Veda’s ninth book. Commentators note that the ritualists worship the Lord (“me”) without knowing it; their prayers are for heaven, and heaven is what they get. Śaṅkara says they are purified by the rites but seek limited ends. Rāmānuja says they enjoy heaven, then return. The verse is in triṣṭubh and describes the joys of heaven vividly: <i>divyān divi deva-bhogān</i>, “divine pleasures of the gods in heaven”.",
 "Good deeds bring good results. Still, ask yourself whether results are all you want.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Te taṃ bhuktvā svarga-lokaṃ viśālaṃ</i>, “having enjoyed that vast heaven”. <i>Kṣīṇe puṇye martya-lokaṃ viśanti</i>, “their merit spent, they enter the mortal world”. <i>Evaṃ trayī-dharmam anuprapannā</i>, “thus following the Vedic dharma”. <i>Gatāgataṃ kāma-kāmā labhante</i>, “desiring desires, they come and go”.",
 "<i>Kṣīṇe puṇye</i>, “when merit is exhausted”, has become a proverbial phrase. Commentators compare heaven to a bank account: when spent, one must return to earn more. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad (5.10.5–6) describes how souls return from the lunar world through rain and food into new births. Śaṅkara says those who desire desires (<i>kāma-kāma</i>) are bound to come and go. Rāmānuja contrasts them with devotees who reach the Lord and do not return. The verse completes the critique of heaven-seeking begun in 2.42–46.",
 "Rewards run out. Invest in what does not: understanding, love, a clear heart.")
DEEP[22]=("<i>Ananyāś cintayanto māṃ</i>, “thinking of me with no other thought”. <i>Ye janāḥ paryupāsate</i>, “those who worship”. <i>Teṣāṃ nityābhiyuktānāṃ</i>, “for them, ever united”. <i>Yoga-kṣemaṃ vahāmy aham</i>, “I carry their welfare”.",
 "This verse is among the most quoted in the Gītā. <i>Yoga-kṣemaṃ vahāmy aham</i> is the motto of the Life Insurance Corporation of India. <i>Yoga</i> here means gaining what one lacks; <i>kṣema</i>, keeping what one has. <i>Vahāmi</i>, “I carry”, is a vivid verb: the Lord personally bears the load. A popular story tells of a scholar who doubted this verse and crossed out <i>vahāmi</i>; that day a boy brought food to his house, with scratches on his back. Rāmānuja says the Lord takes care of everything for those who depend on him alone. Compare 2.45, where anxiety about yoga-kṣema is to be dropped.",
 "Do your part, and trust the rest to a larger care. It frees the mind for love.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Ye’py anya-devatā-bhaktā</i>, “even those devoted to other gods”. <i>Yajante śraddhayānvitāḥ</i>, “who worship with faith”. <i>Te’pi mām eva kaunteya</i>, “they too, me alone, son of Kuntī”. <i>Yajanty avidhi-pūrvakam</i>, “worship, though not by the proper way”.",
 "Commentators find this verse generous but qualified. All sincere worship reaches the one Lord; but <i>avidhi-pūrvakam</i>, “not in the proper way”, means without knowing who the true recipient is. Śaṅkara says the proper way is knowing the Lord as the Self of all. Rāmānuja says other gods are the Lord’s body; worship of them reaches him, but limited understanding limits the fruit. Modern readers debate how far the verse extends to other religions. Many Hindu teachers, such as Vivekananda, used it to support respect for all faiths.",
 "Sincere worship anywhere moves toward the Highest. Let that soften your view of others’ faith.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Ahaṃ hi sarva-yajñānāṃ</i>, “for I, of all sacrifices”. <i>Bhoktā ca prabhur eva ca</i>, “am the enjoyer and the Lord”. <i>Na tu mām abhijānanti</i>, “but they do not know me”. <i>Tattvenātaś cyavanti te</i>, “in truth, and so they fall”.",
 "<i>Cyavanti</i>, “they fall”, means they fall back into the cycle of birth after their reward is spent. Commentators explain: the offering reaches the Lord, but not knowing this, the worshipper seeks only limited fruits and so gets only them. Śaṅkara says knowing the Lord as the enjoyer of all sacrifices leads beyond rebirth. Rāmānuja says such knowledge transforms ritual into devotion. The verse echoes 5.29, where the Lord is called <i>bhoktā yajña-tapasām</i>, enjoyer of sacrifices and austerities.",
 "Know whom you serve. It changes the service, and where it leads.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Yānti deva-vratā devān</i>, “devotees of the gods go to the gods”. <i>Pitṝn yānti pitṛ-vratāḥ</i>, “devotees of ancestors go to the ancestors”. <i>Bhūtāni yānti bhūtejyā</i>, “worshippers of spirits go to the spirits”. <i>Yānti mad-yājino’pi mām</i>, “my worshippers come to me”.",
 "Commentators call this the law of destination: you go where your devotion points. <i>Deva</i>, the gods of heaven; <i>pitṛ</i>, the ancestors, honoured in śrāddha rites; <i>bhūta</i>, spirits or elemental beings. Śaṅkara notes that the effort may be the same, but the goal differs. Rāmānuja says the Lord gives each what they seek. The verse links to 8.6, where the last thought determines the next state. What we worship over a lifetime shapes what we become.",
 "Whatever you give your heart to, you are moving toward. Choose with care.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Patraṃ puṣpaṃ phalaṃ toyaṃ</i>, “a leaf, a flower, a fruit, water”. <i>Yo me bhaktyā prayacchati</i>, “whoever offers me with devotion”. <i>Tad ahaṃ bhakty-upahṛtam</i>, “that offering of love”. <i>Aśnāmi prayatātmanaḥ</i>, “I accept from the pure-hearted”.",
 "This is perhaps the most loved verse of devotion in the Gītā. The offerings are the simplest possible, available to the poorest person. Devotees tell several stories. When Kṛṣṇa visited Vidura, Vidura’s wife, overwhelmed, peeled bananas and gave him the peels, and he ate them happily. Sudāmā, his poor childhood friend, brought a handful of flattened rice, and Kṛṣṇa ate it with joy and made him rich. Draupadī fed him a single leaf from her empty pot, and his hunger, and that of a visiting sage’s disciples, was satisfied. <i>Aśnāmi</i>, “I eat”: the Lord actually receives it. What matters is <i>bhakti</i>, love.",
 "You don’t need wealth to offer. A leaf, a flower, a glass of water, given with love, is enough.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Yat karoṣi yad aśnāsi</i>, “whatever you do, whatever you eat”. <i>Yaj juhoṣi dadāsi yat</i>, “whatever you offer, whatever you give”. <i>Yat tapasyasi kaunteya</i>, “whatever austerity you practise, son of Kuntī”. <i>Tat kuruṣva mad-arpaṇam</i>, “do it as an offering to me”.",
 "Commentators see this as the simplest complete practice in the Gītā. Five areas of life are named: action, eating, worship, giving and discipline. Each is to be offered. Śaṅkara says this purifies the mind for knowledge. Rāmānuja says it is the core of bhakti in action. Many Hindu households say “Kṛṣṇārpaṇam astu”, “may this be offered to Kṛṣṇa”, after completing a task. The verse joins karma yoga and bhakti: work becomes worship, and worship becomes the whole of life.",
 "Before starting work or a meal, quietly offer it. Over time this small habit changes everything.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Śubhāśubha-phalair evaṃ</i>, “thus from good and bad fruits”. <i>Mokṣyase karma-bandhanaiḥ</i>, “you will be freed from the bonds of action”. <i>Sannyāsa-yoga-yuktātmā</i>, “your self united in the yoga of renunciation”. <i>Vimukto mām upaiṣyasi</i>, “freed, you will come to me”.",
 "Commentators note that offering everything is itself renunciation (<i>sannyāsa</i>). You give up ownership of the fruit by giving it to the Lord. Śaṅkara says this frees one from merit and demerit alike, both of which bind. Rāmānuja says offering action to the Lord is the renunciation the Gītā asks for. The verse resolves the long debate of chapters 3–6 between action and renunciation: offer the action, and you have renounced it while still doing it.",
 "Offer the result before you start. Its weight lifts, and the work grows lighter.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Samo’haṃ sarva-bhūteṣu</i>, “I am the same in all beings”. <i>Na me dveṣyo’sti na priyaḥ</i>, “none is hateful to me, none dear”. <i>Ye bhajanti tu māṃ bhaktyā</i>, “but those who worship me with devotion”. <i>Mayi te teṣu cāpy aham</i>, “they are in me, and I in them”.",
 "This verse seems to contradict 7.17 and 12.13–20, where devotees are called dear. Commentators resolve it. Śaṅkara’s image is fire: it gives warmth equally, but only those who come near feel it. Rāmānuja says the Lord has no partiality in giving, but the devotee’s love creates intimacy, a mutual indwelling. The sun shines on all, but only an open window lets it in. The phrase <i>mayi te teṣu cāpy aham</i>, “they in me and I in them”, is one of the Gītā’s most intimate.",
 "The Divine is equally near to all. Love is what opens the window.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Api cet su-durācāro</i>, “even if a very wicked person”. <i>Bhajate mām ananya-bhāk</i>, “worships me with undivided devotion”. <i>Sādhur eva sa mantavyaḥ</i>, “he should be considered good”. <i>Samyag vyavasito hi saḥ</i>, “for he has rightly resolved”.",
 "Commentators find this one of the most hopeful verses in any scripture. The story of Vālmīki is often told: a robber who, by repeating Rāma’s name, became the sage who wrote the Rāmāyaṇa. Aṅgulimāla in the Buddhist tradition and Ajāmila in the Bhāgavata are similar. Śaṅkara says right resolve itself is the beginning of goodness. Rāmānuja says the Lord accepts such a person because he has turned rightly. The verse does not excuse wrongdoing; it says the turning has begun, and that matters.",
 "No one is beyond change. The moment of sincere turning is already a new beginning.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Kṣipraṃ bhavati dharmātmā</i>, “he soon becomes righteous”. <i>Śaśvac-chāntiṃ nigacchati</i>, “attains lasting peace”. <i>Kaunteya pratijānīhi</i>, “son of Kuntī, declare boldly”. <i>Na me bhaktaḥ praṇaśyati</i>, “my devotee never perishes”.",
 "Why does Kṛṣṇa ask Arjuna to declare it, rather than declaring it himself? Commentators offer an answer: the Lord sometimes breaks his own word for his devotees (as when he took up a weapon against Bhīṣma, breaking his vow not to fight), but he never lets a devotee’s word fail. So he asks Arjuna to make the promise. <i>Na me bhaktaḥ praṇaśyati</i> is inscribed in many temples and homes. Rāmānuja calls it a solemn guarantee.",
 "Devotion is a safe road. Walk it without fear.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Māṃ hi pārtha vyapāśritya</i>, “taking refuge in me, Pārtha”. <i>Ye’pi syuḥ pāpa-yonayaḥ</i>, “even those of humble birth”. <i>Striyo vaiśyās tathā śūdrās</i>, “women, vaiśyas and śūdras”. <i>Te’pi yānti parāṃ gatim</i>, “they too reach the supreme goal”.",
 "This verse has been read in different ways. Traditional commentators, including Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja, took <i>pāpa-yoni</i>, “of sinful birth”, as a separate category, and saw the verse as opening liberation to those barred from Vedic study, including women, vaiśyas and śūdras. Some commentators read <i>pāpa-yonayaḥ</i> as describing the groups that follow. Many modern readers object to the phrasing, which reflects the social views of its time, while honouring the verse’s message of inclusion. The bhakti movements took this message seriously: many of their greatest saints were women or from communities excluded from Vedic learning.",
 "Whatever the old wording, the heart of this verse is that no one is shut out from the Divine.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Kiṃ punar brāhmaṇāḥ puṇyā</i>, “how much more, then, holy Brahmins”. <i>Bhaktā rājarṣayas tathā</i>, “and devoted royal sages”. <i>Anityam asukhaṃ lokam</i>, “this impermanent, joyless world”. <i>Imaṃ prāpya bhajasva mām</i>, “having come into, worship me”.",
 "<i>Anityam asukham</i>, impermanent and without lasting joy. Commentators note that this is not despair but realism: the world cannot give permanent happiness, so use this human life to seek what does. Śaṅkara says the rarity of human birth makes it precious. Rāmānuja says Arjuna, a royal sage, is especially fit. <i>Imaṃ prāpya</i>, “having reached this”, reminds that a human birth is a rare chance. Indian tradition often says that among all births, the human one is best for spiritual growth.",
 "This life passes quickly. Use some of it, every day, for what lasts.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Man-manā bhava mad-bhakto</i>, “fix your mind on me, be devoted to me”. <i>Mad-yājī māṃ namaskuru</i>, “worship me, bow to me”. <i>Mām evaiṣyasi yuktvaivam</i>, “thus united, you will come to me”. <i>Ātmānaṃ mat-parāyaṇaḥ</i>, “with me as your goal”.",
 "This verse returns almost word for word as 18.65, near the end of the Gītā, with the added promise “I promise you truly, for you are dear to me”. Commentators see it as the essence of bhakti in four steps: mind, heart, action and body. Śaṅkara reads it as the culmination of devotion leading to knowledge. Rāmānuja reads it as the full practice of bhakti yoga. Madhva stresses total dedication to Viṣṇu. The colophon names chapter 9 Rāja-Vidyā Rāja-Guhya Yoga. Chapter 10 will describe the Lord’s glories.",
 "Turn your mind, your love, your work and your body toward the Highest. That is the whole path.")
